        ## Interrupts (GIC interrupt IDs, data sheet Table 3-16)

        The interrupt controller is the GIC of the Cortex-A9 (96 sources; IDs 0-31 are private to the A9, IDs 32-95 are shared peripheral interrupts, ID = 32 + SPI number). The table is machine-translated in the data sheet: `SSP` is the SPI, `2C` the I2C, `AND` the IR, `HOURS` the SATA. The original Chinese table (user guide v02, Table 3-16) names ID 65 VOIE and ID 72 VEDU (H.264 encoder).

        | ID | Source | ID | Source | ID | Source |
        |----|--------|----|--------|----|--------|
        | 32 | COMMTX[0] | 52 | SATA | 72 | VEDU |
        | 33 | COMMRX[0] | 53 | USB EHCI | 73 | JPGE |
        | 34 | WDG | 54 | USB OHCI | 74 | JPGD |
        | 35 | Timer0 | 55 | CIPHER | 75 | Reserved in the data sheet (SCD per vendor code) |
        | 36 | Timer1 | 56 | ETH | 76 | MD |
        | 37 | TIMER2 | 57 | FE PHY | 77 | IVE |
        | 38 | TIMER3 | 58-59 | Reserved | 78 | Reserved |
        | 39 | RTC | 60 | Software interrupt | 79-87 | GPIO0-GPIO8 |
        | 40-43 | UART0-UART3 | 61 | L2CACHE_CHK0_INT | 88 | Reserved |
        | 44 | SPI | 62 | L2CACHE_CHK1_INT | 89 | TEM_CAP_INT (RTC temperature sensor) |
        | 45 | I2C | 63 | L2CACHE_INT_COMB | 90-95 | Reserved |
        | 46 | IR | 64 | A9_PMU_INT0 | | |
        | 47 | AIO | 65 | VOIE | | |
        | 48-49 | Reserved | 66 | HDMI | | |
        | 50 | SFC | 67 | VDP | | |
        | 51 | Reserved | 68-71 | VICAP, VPSS, TDE, VCMP | | |

        VDH/SCD interrupts (second pass 2026-11-10, vendor module hi3520D_vfmw.ko, not verified on hardware): ID 75 = SCD (confidence MEDIUM: the vendor driver requests it for the SCD service routine; the data sheet lists it as reserved). The VDH0/VDH1 decoders have NO known ID: no vendor build requests an interrupt for them (confidence HIGH for that statement); data sheet reserved IDs that could be wired: 48, 49, 51, 58, 59, 78, 88, 90-95. The Hi3535 and Hi3536 vfmw modules request VDH IDs (67/72 and 65/66/123) but those chips have a different interrupt map. Probe with the GIC pending registers (see docs/vdh-driver-design.md).

        UART0-UART3 are PL011s without modem-control or IrDA signals: the data sheet (14.3) lists UART_FR bits [2:0] (CTS/DSR/DCD) and [8] (RI), UART_CR bits [13:12] (OUT1/OUT2) and [2:1] (SIR), the modem interrupt bits [3:0] of UART_IMSC/UART_RIS/UART_MIS/UART_ICR and the UART_ILPR register as reserved. The nUARTRTS/nUARTCTS flow-control bits of UART_CR exist (UART1 has RTS/CTS pins).

        The table names IDs 35-38 Timer0-Timer3 and has no entries for Timer4-Timer7. Section 3.6 says the two timers of a Dual-Timer module share one interrupt line, so each of IDs 35-38 is the line of one Dual-Timer module (Dual-Timer0 = Timer0+1 at 0x20000000, Dual-Timer1 = Timer2+3 at 0x20010000, Dual-Timer2 = Timer4+5 at 0x20130000, Dual-Timer3 = Timer6+7 at 0x20140000); the original data sheet (3.6.1) says each pair shares one base address and one interrupt line.

        &#9989; = registers described; &#9888; partial = partly described (offsets known from vendor U-Boot headers or a sibling chip, many bit meanings and all reset values unknown; the block desc starts with 'Partly described:' and registers/fields whose meaning is guessed say 'Meaning unverified'); &#9888; not described = placeholder only (the block's registers exist only in closed vendor libraries and are not documented anywhere; the map only holds an undocumented-window placeholder, the block desc starts with 'Not described:'). Registers whose desc starts with 'Undocumented:' are known to exist (seen in the vendor boot register table) but their meaning is unknown.

        <!-- overview:begin -->

        ### Diagrams

        ![Logic block diagram](assets/diagrams/hi3515av100-logic.svg)

        ![Register blocks by function](assets/diagrams/hi3515av100-blocks.svg)

        ![Interrupt topology](assets/diagrams/hi3515av100-irq.svg)

        ![Reset tree](assets/diagrams/hi3515av100-resets.svg)

        ![Power domains](assets/diagrams/hi3515av100-power.svg)

        ![CRG register map](assets/diagrams/hi3515av100-crgmap.svg)

        ![bus clock domains](assets/diagrams/hi3515av100-bus.svg)

        ![clock tree](assets/diagrams/hi3515av100-clktree.svg)

        ![IP edition per chip](assets/diagrams/hi3515av100-family.svg)

        ### Variants (data sheet chapter 1 and 2; the data sheet is Chinese, translated)

        One data sheet covers the Hi3520D, Hi3515A and Hi3515C (all V100): one address map, one CRG/SYS_CTRL/IO-config register set and one interrupt table. The register map is documented once; the three chips differ in the figures below.

        | | Hi3520D V100 | Hi3515A V100 | Hi3515C V100 |
        |---|---|---|---|
        | CPU | Cortex-A9, up to 660 MHz | Cortex-A9, 600 MHz | Cortex-A9, 600 MHz |
        | Caches | 32 KB L1 I + 32 KB L1 D, 128 KB L2 | same | same |
        | DDR | one 16-bit DDR2/DDR3 controller, up to 660 MHz, up to 512 MB | same, up to 660 MHz | same, up to 600 MHz |
        | SATA 2.5 | 2 | 2 | 1 |
        | H.264 decode | 8xD1@30, 4x720p@30, 2x1080p@30 | 4xD1@30, 2x720p@30, 1x1080p@30 | same as Hi3515A |
        | H.264 encode + decode mix | 4xD1@30 + 4xCIF@30 enc + 4xD1@30 dec (and the other mixes of the data sheet) | 4xD1@30 + 4xCIF@30 enc + 1xD1@30 dec | same as Hi3515A |
        | Video input | 2xBT.656 @ 108/144 MHz (8xCIF/D1/960H), or 2xBT.656 @ 148.5 MHz (2x720p), or 1xBT.1120 @ 148.5 MHz (1x1080p) | 1xBT.656 @ 108/144 MHz (4xCIF/D1/960H), or 1xBT.656 @ 148.5 MHz (1x720p) | same as Hi3515A |
        | Video output | HDMI 1.3 + VGA + 2xCVBS (HDMI and VGA same source), up to 1080p@60 | same | same |
        | Common | 1 FE Ethernet (internal FE PHY, MDI or RMII), 4 UART, 1 SPI (2 CS), IR, I2C, GPIO, 2 USB 2.0 host, 2 I2S, SPI NOR (1/2/4 bit, 2 CS), 4 KB BOOTROM + 10 KB SRAM, RTC with temperature sensor, AES/DES/3DES, IVE | | |
        | Package | Epad-LQFP256, 28 x 28 mm, 0.4 mm pitch | same | same |
        | Supplies | core 1.25 V, I/O 3.3 V, DDR 1.8/1.5 V; 2.5 W typical (Hi3520D) | | |

        ### Boot (data sheet 1.3)

        * BOOT_SEL (pin AIO_MCLK) = 1: boot from the on-chip BOOTROM. The BOOTROM starts the serial download mechanism (Fastboot tool over the UART); if the serial link times out without a response it continues with the SPI flash.
        * BOOT_SEL = 0: boot directly from the SPI NOR flash on the SFC.
        * Address 0x00000000 aliases the boot memory (64 MB window) until the remap is cleared, after which it points to the on-chip RAM.

        ### Clocks (data sheet CRG chapter)

        The crystal on XIN feeds the PLLs (APLL = PERI_CRG0/1, VPLL0 = PERI_CRG2/3, EPLL = PERI_CRG8/9, each with a bypass, level-1/level-2 post dividers and an integer/fractional multiplier). PERI_CRG10 holds the A9 frequency mode, PERI_CRG11..62 gate the peripheral clocks and hold the soft resets (generated map below). XIN also reaches the IP clock frequency controller directly; VIUn_CLK are the external video input clocks. Diagram: the data sheet CRG block diagram, redrawn.

        ### Chip ID

        | Chip | System controller ID registers (0xEE0 .. 0xEEC) | Value read as SC_SYSID |
        |---|---|---|
        | hi3520dv100 | SYSID0..3 reset values from the RDL | 0x3520D100 |
        | hi3515av100 | SYSID0..3 reset values from the RDL | 0x3520D100 |
        | hi3515cv100 | SYSID0..3 reset values from the RDL | 0x3520D100 |

        The four 8-bit registers form the 32-bit chip ID (SYSID3 is the most significant byte). The values are the reset values of the register descriptions: one data sheet documents one ID for all chips it covers, so the chips of a shared data sheet are not distinguished here (the real value of each variant is not stated).

        ### Memory map notes (data sheet 1.3.3, Table 1-1)

        * 0x00000000-0x03FFFFFF (64 MB) is the boot window: while the address remap is active it points to the boot address space (BOOTROM when booted from the BOOTROM, the SPI flash memory space when booted from SPI flash); after the remap is cleared (SYS_CTRL) it points to the on-chip RAM.
        * BOOTROM window 0x04000000 (64 KB window, 4 KB implemented); on-chip RAM window 0x04010000 (64 KB window, 10 KB implemented).
        * SPI flash memory space (SFC_MEM window): 0x58000000-0x5BFFFFFF, 64 MB. The SFC registers are at 0x10010000.
        * DDR window: 0x80000000-0xBFFFFFFF (1 GB window; the device is at most 512 MB, key specifications). 0xC0000000-0xFFFFFFFF is reserved.
        * AHB peripherals 0x10000000-0x100FFFFF (SFC, SATA, ETH, USB OHCI/EHCI, CIPHER); APB peripherals and system blocks 0x20000000-0x201FFFFF (timers, CRG, WDG, SYS_CTRL, RTC, IR, UARTs, SPI, I2C, IO config, DDRC, PWM, GPIO0-8, HDMI); media blocks 0x20580000-0x206CFFFF (VICAP, VDP, IVE, VPSS, TDE, VEDU, VOIE, AIO, JPGE, JPGD, VCMP, MD).
        * Cortex-A9 private region 0x20300000 (64 KB: SCU, GIC, global/private timers, watchdog), L2 cache controller 0x20700000 (1 MB window), ARM DEBUG 0x20400000-0x2051FFFF (1152 KB), DDR test module (DDRT) 0x206D0000.
        * The 0x100D0000-0x1014FFFF range is reserved: the chip has no DMA controller (see DMA below).

        ### DMA request lines

        The Hi3520D, Hi3515A and Hi3515C have no DMA controller: the data sheet has no DMAC chapter, no DMAC interrupt and no DMAC clock gate (memory map: 0x100D0000-0x1014FFFF reserved). Bulk data masters instead have their own bus-master interfaces (SFC DMA read/write, SATA AHCI, ETH, USB EHCI, CIPHER, video blocks); UART, SPI and I2C run in PIO mode.

        ### Pin multiplexing summary (IO_CONFIG at 0x200F0000)

        Generated from the pin mux registers of this description (register offsets are relative to the block base; the function lists are the value-ordered choices of each register). Pads that repeat a pattern in consecutive registers are merged into one row.

        | Pads | Mux register (offset from 0x200F0000) | Function choices (value 0, 1, ...) |
        |---|---|---|
        | VI_ADC_CLK | muxctrl_reg0 @0x000 | 0=GPIO8_5 / 1=VI_ADC_CLK / 2=CLK_TEST_OUT0 / 3=CLK_TEST_OUT1 / 4=CLK_TEST_OUT2 / 5=CLK_TEST_OUT3 / 6=RTC_TEST_CLK |
        | VIU0_CLK ... VIU0_DAT7 (9 pads) | muxctrl_reg1 @0x004 | 0=PRIMARY / 1=GPIO / 2=FEPHY_DBG / 3=NEXT_PIN |
        | VIU1_CLK ... VIU1_DAT7 (9 pads) | muxctrl_reg2 @0x008 | 0=PRIMARY / 1=GPIO / 2=FEPHY_DBG / 3=NEXT_PIN |
        | VGA_HS | muxctrl_reg3 @0x00C | 0=GPIO2_0 / 1=VGA_HS |
        | VGA_VS | muxctrl_reg4 @0x010 | 0=GPIO1_5 / 1=VGA_VS |
        | AIO_MCLK | muxctrl_reg5 @0x014 | 0=AIO_MCLK / 1=GPIO7_0 / 2=BOOT_SEL |
        | AIO_BCLK_TX | muxctrl_reg6 @0x018 | 0=AIO_BCLK_TX / 1=GPIO7_1 |
        | AIO_WS_TX | muxctrl_reg7 @0x01C | 0=AIO_WS_TX / 1=GPIO7_2 |
        | AIO_SD_TX | muxctrl_reg8 @0x020 | 0=AIO_SD_TX / 1=GPIO7_3 / 2=JTAG_SEL |
        | AIO_BCLK_RX | muxctrl_reg9 @0x024 | 0=AIO_BCLK_RX / 1=GPIO7_4 |
        | AIO_WS_RX | muxctrl_reg10 @0x028 | 0=AIO_WS_RX / 1=GPIO7_5 |
        | AIO_SD_RX | muxctrl_reg11 @0x02C | 0=AIO_SD_RX / 1=GPIO7_6 |
        | SPI_SCLK | muxctrl_reg12 @0x030 | 0=GPIO8_0 / 1=SPI_SCLK |
        | SPI_SDO | muxctrl_reg13 @0x034 | 0=PLL_TEST_OUT0 / 1=SPI_SDO / 2=PLL_TEST_OUT1 / 3=PLL_TEST_OUT2 / 4=GPIO8_1 |
        | SPI_SDI | muxctrl_reg14 @0x038 | 0=GPIO8_2 / 1=SPI_SDI |
        | SPI_CSN0 | muxctrl_reg15 @0x03C | 0=GPIO8_3 / 1=SPI_CSN0 |
        | SPI_CSN1 | muxctrl_reg16 @0x040 | 0=GPIO8_4 / 1=SPI_CSN1 / 2=PWM_SVB |
        | I2C_SDA | muxctrl_reg17 @0x044 | 0=GPIO1_6 / 1=I2C_SDA |
        | I2C_SCL | muxctrl_reg18 @0x048 | 0=GPIO1_7 / 1=I2C_SCL |
        | UART1_RTSN | muxctrl_reg19 @0x04C | 0=GPIO5_0 / 1=UART1_RTSN |
        | UART1_RXD | muxctrl_reg20 @0x050 | 0=GPIO5_1 / 1=UART1_RXD |
        | UART1_CTSN | muxctrl_reg21 @0x054 | 0=GPIO5_2 / 1=UART1_CTSN / 2=PWM_SVB |
        | UART1_TXD | muxctrl_reg22 @0x058 | 0=GPIO5_3 / 1=UART1_TXD |
        | UART2_RXD | muxctrl_reg23 @0x05C | 0=GPIO5_4 / 1=UART2_RXD |
        | UART2_TXD | muxctrl_reg24 @0x060 | 0=GPIO5_5 / 1=UART2_TXD |
        | IR_IN | muxctrl_reg25 @0x064 | 0=GPIO7_7 / 1=IR_IN |
